## GP audit update — dlopen_probe misclassifies waitpid failure as SAFE

#### [GP-APK-010] waitpid error/EINTR leaves zero-initialized status, which is then interpreted as child exit code 0
Status: CONFIRMED control-flow defect; signal-interruption reproducer PENDING
Severity: P1
File/function: `src/jni/dlopen_exec.cpp` — `dlopen_probe` (~320–340).
Root cause:
```cpp
int st = 0;
while (waitpid(p, &st, 0) < 0) {
    // EINTR only; other errors would leave the child unreaped — accept.
    break;
}
...
if (WIFSIGNALED(st)) return FAULTED;
if (WIFEXITED(st) && WEXITSTATUS(st) == 0) return SAFE;
```
The loop breaks for EINTR instead of retrying, and also breaks for every other waitpid error. Since `st` was initialized to zero, the subsequent macros interpret it as a normal exit status 0 (`WIFEXITED(0)` is true and `WEXITSTATUS(0)==0`). The probe therefore returns SAFE without having observed the child result.
Trigger: a signal interrupts waitpid before the child exits, or SIGCHLD is ignored/another reaper consumes the child and waitpid returns ECHILD.
Impact: a library that actually faulted or hung in the probe can be misclassified as SAFE, so the parent proceeds to real dlopen and reintroduces the loader crash the probe is supposed to prevent.
Generic fix: loop on EINTR; for any other waitpid failure return an explicit UNKNOWN/REFUSED/FAULTED state (never SAFE). Track a separate `wait_ok` boolean and only inspect `st` after successful waitpid. Add a timeout/kill path so a hung child does not block the runtime forever.
Tests required: waitpid success exit 0, exit nonzero, signal death, EINTR injected before child completion, ECHILD/SIGCHLD=SIG_IGN, and timeout; only a successfully reaped exit-0 child may return SAFE.
